        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "859e1c1c-d9c9-5bf5-bdfa-f1b9172508c9",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Ghana, Winneba, Elubo, Sekondi-Takoradi, Cape Coast, Accra, Tema, Aflao, Ivory Coast, Togo, Bahamas\n- Systems: N1, National Highway 1\n- Events: Fancy Dress Festival, Junkanoo, Kakamotobi\n- Topics: fishing ports, masquerade festivals, street parades\n\nFacts:\n- The N1, or National Highway 1, is a national highway in Ghana that begins at Elubo on the Ivory Coast border and runs through Sekondi-Takoradi, Cape Coast, Winneba, Accra, and Tema to Aflao on the Togo border.\n- Winneba, traditionally known as Simpa, is a historic fishing port on the south coast of Ghana, 90 miles east of Cape Coast.\n- The Fancy Dress Festival, locally called Kakamotobi, is a masquerade festival held every year in Winneba in Ghana's Central Region from Christmas to the first day of January.\n- Junkanoo is a street parade with music, dance, and costumes of Akan origin held in many towns across the Bahamas every Boxing Day and New Year's Day, and it is the same as Kakamotobi or the Fancy Dress Festival.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "62093821-bea0-57cc-b56b-6d26961f5eb1",
        "text": "Page: N1 road (Ghana). Sentence: The N1 or National Highway 1 is a national highway in Ghana that begins at the border with Ivory Coast at Elubo and runs through Sekondi-Takoradi, Cape Coast, Winneba, Accra and Tema to the border with Togo at Aflao.\nPage: Winneba. Sentence: Winneba, traditionally known as \"Simpa\", is a historic fishing port in south Ghana, lying on the south coast, 90 mi east of Cape Coast.\nPage: Fancy Dress Festival. Sentence: The Fancy Dress Festival(known locally as \"Kakamotobi\") is a masquerade festival held on Christmas to the first day of January every year by the people of Winneba in the Central region of Ghana.\nPage: Junkanoo. Sentence: Junkanoo is a street parade with music, dance, and costumes of Akan origin in many towns across the Bahamas every Boxing Day (December 26) and New Year's Day (January 1), the same as \"Kakamotobi\" or the Fancy Dress Festival.",
